[{:db/id 17592186179718 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8917ffe4-7713-5b06-82b5-c913e5f2e407"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8917ffe4-7713-5b06-82b5-c913e5f2e407" :question/id #uuid "8917ffe4-7713-5b06-82b5-c913e5f2e407" :question/math-academy-id "q-122880" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=6x+2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get $6x+2y=-1,$ which gives the line $y=-3x-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $6x+2y=0,$ which gives the line $y=-3x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get $6x+2y=2,$ which gives the line $y=-3x+1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d6c45af410df1ae974062b777b856b3960e72ad13e14d37a48e5a92cebf74cd1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as both $x$ and  $y$ increase. \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa601a2bb31bb8c009f554361a79135daf1d7abd2779eaf1ae8b2431a01eafa.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4cef7ae1-6ba6-5fd3-82d2-e1546e8de597"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4cef7ae1-6ba6-5fd3-82d2-e1546e8de597" :answer-field/id #uuid "4cef7ae1-6ba6-5fd3-82d2-e1546e8de597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eeddcc87-ac3b-50d2-be15-6abdd930a045" :answer-field/choices ["answer-758d7c9c-7190-58ba-88ca-6ba4209fb9ec" "answer-b6af969e-